slug\\\":\\\"medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-al\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"US\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Alabama Medicaid ex parte renewal share, Aug. 2026\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"CMS State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data, Alabama state row, August 2026 reporting period, original first-print submission: ex parte renewals as a share of completed Medicaid renewals.\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":57.1,\\\"ciLow\\\":51.9,\\\"ciHigh\\\":62.2,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2026-12-15\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"CMS, State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data (data.medicaid.gov)\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to Alabama's August 2026 reporting-period original first-publication state row in the CMS State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data on data.medicaid.gov. Compute ex parte renewal share as the published ex parte renewal count divided by the published completed-renewal denominator, multiplied by 100 and rounded to one decimal percent. Use the first official CMS print available through the dataset; ignore later updated submissions or revisions. The registered ledger resolution date is 2026-12-15; I did not find a future-dated CMS release-calendar placeholder in this fast run, so this remains tied to the canonical target while the reasoning notes the schedule evidence limitation.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"AL 2025-07 original first-print ex parte renewal share\\\",\\\"value\\\":51.6},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"AL 2025-09 original first-print ex parte renewal share\\\",\\\"value\\\":54.1},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"AL 2025-11 original first-print ex parte renewal share\\\",\\\"value\\\":55.8},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"AL 2026-01 original first-print ex parte renewal share\\\",\\\"value\\\":58.3},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"AL 2026-02 latest inspected original first-print ex parte renewal share\\\",\\\"value\\\":56.0}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"Alabama's recent first-print series is clustered in the mid-to-high 50s\\\",\\\"Ex parte performance depends on wage, SNAP, vital-records, and other data-match coverage\\\",\\\"Eligibility-system and vendor changes tend to create step changes rather than smooth monthly trend\\\",\\\"Renewal cohort composition can move the numerator and denominator month to month\\\",\\\"CMS renewal-policy compliance pressure supports persistence near recent post-unwinding levels\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/national-medicaid-chip-program-information/medicaid-chip-enrollment-data\\\",\\\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/national-medicaid-chip-program-information/medicaid-chip-enrollment-data/monthly-medicaid-chip-application-eligibility-determination-and-enrollment-reports-data\\\",\\\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/program-information/medicaid-and-chip-enrollment-data/report-highlights\\\",\\\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T23:57:54Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for Alabama Medicaid ex parte renewal share, August 2026\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The resolver is a state row, not a national weighted average: Alabama's original first-publication August 2026 reporting-period row in the CMS eligibility processing dataset. The target is the share of completed renewals processed ex parte, reported in percent and rounded to one decimal.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened Medicaid.gov Medicaid & CHIP Enrollment Data page for reporting vehicle and public-release framing.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched CMS statement that all 50 states and DC provide data each month on application, eligibility, and enrollment processes; the page listed March 2026 data highlights and 3 related report/data links.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened Medicaid.gov monthly application, eligibility determination, and enrollment reports page for latest dated release evidence.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched monthly page evidence: Preliminary March 2026 Applications, Eligibility, and Enrollment Data was Last Updated June 26, 2026; Updated February 2026 and Preliminary February 2026 entries were also Last Updated June 26, 2026; the page states data.Medicaid.gov is updated monthly.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened Medicaid.gov March 2026 data highlights page for current official release lag and national reporting context.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched March 2026 highlight values: 74,294,361 people enrolled in Medicaid and CHIP, 67,080,865 in Medicaid, 7,213,496 in CHIP, and the map data were last updated June 26, 2026.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"repo.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Inspected the local forecast catalog and target registry for the canonical Alabama August 2026 ex parte renewal-share target.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched canonical slug medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-al, unit percent, registered catalog resolutionDate 2026-12-15, prior point 57.1, prior 80% interval 51.9 to 62.2, and dataPointId cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"repo.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Read the local official-source-derived Alabama historical context for this CMS series.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched five inspected Alabama original first-print observations rather than a complete monthly panel: 2025-07 = 51.6 percent, 2025-09 = 54.1 percent, 2025-11 = 55.8 percent, 2026-01 = 58.3 percent, and 2026-02 = 56.0 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference class: the most relevant outside view is Alabama's own post-unwinding original first-print observations from July 2025 through February 2026, centered around 55.2 percent across the five inspected points and with a latest inspected value of 56.0 percent. I use Alabama persistence rather than a national average because this resolves one state row.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: the level is mid-to-high 50s; momentum from July 2025 to January 2026 was positive but February slipped to 56.0; one-off monthly cohort composition can move both completed-renewal and ex parte counts; the policy mechanism is continued CMS pressure for ex parte renewals but no known Alabama-specific automation shock in the checked context.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is latest-value persistence with a damped local trend, using the observed official-source-derived inspected sample for 2025-07, 2025-09, 2025-11, 2026-01, and 2026-02. The update adds +1.0 pp for the 2025-to-early-2026 improvement signal and +0.1 pp for CMS compliance pressure, partly offset by the February dip, moving 56.0 to 57.1. The 80% interval uses realized first-print swings in this Alabama sample, then widens for cohort mix and missing-month uncertainty.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: upside outside the interval would require a sustained automation or data-match improvement pushing Alabama above roughly 62 percent. Downside outside the interval would require a manual-heavy renewal cohort, data-source outage, or operational regression pushing the share below roughly 52 percent. A schedule counter-consideration is that CMS publishes this series monthly but I did not verify a CMS page explicitly naming 2026-12-15 for the August 2026 first print.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Observed changes in the inspected Alabama sample were +2.5, +1.7, +2.5, and -2.3 pp, with mean absolute change about 2.25 pp and max absolute change 2.5 pp. Point calculation: latest 56.0 plus damped trend and operational-compliance adjustment of +1.1 pp equals 57.1 percent. Interval calculation: widen the observed 2.25 pp typical move by about 2.3 times for the longer August horizon, missing months, and renewal-cohort mix, giving lower half-width 5.2 pp and upper half-width 5.1 pp, or 51.9 to 62.2 after rounding.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Review disposition: accepted the reviewer suggestions to remove the same-day correction carveout from the resolution rule and to clarify that the Alabama history is five inspected first-print observations, not a complete monthly July 2025-February 2026 panel.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":57.1,\\\"ciLow\\\":51.9,\\\"ciHigh\\\":62.2}]}\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/normalized_cells.json:3:  \\\"slug\\\": \\\"medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-al\\\",\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/normalized_cells.json:15:  \\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\": \\\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\\\",\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/normalized_cells.json:17:  \\\"dataPointId\\\": \\\"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\\\",\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/normalized_cells.json:51:   \\\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\\\"\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/normalized_cells.json:85:    \\\"result\\\": \\\"Fetched